WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.509
Symphony can give its agents more tools through MCP servers.

s1.2
00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:08.143
A server can search the web, read a wiki, or look up an error tracker.

s1.3
00:00:08.143 --> 00:00:11.528
An admin picks the servers, and the agent roles that get them.

s1.4
00:00:11.528 --> 00:00:16.256
When an agent starts, Symphony writes its role's servers into the agent's private config.

s1.5
00:00:16.256 --> 00:00:19.542
The agent sees each tool next to its usual tools.

s2.1
00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:22.245
A server uses one of three transports.

s2.2
00:00:22.245 --> 00:00:27.184
Streamable HTTP and the older SSE transport reach a remote server.

s2.3
00:00:27.184 --> 00:00:31.153
A stdio server is a process that runs next to the agent.

s2.4
00:00:31.153 --> 00:00:33.518
Each server lists the agent roles that get it.

s2.5
00:00:33.518 --> 00:00:36.810
A role without servers gets no MCP tools.

s3.1
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.302
A remote server points to the sandbox gateway, not to the real URL.

s3.2
00:00:41.302 --> 00:00:45.185
Each agent session mints its own token, for its session and its role.

s3.3
00:00:45.185 --> 00:00:48.707
Redis keeps only a hash of it, and the agent sends it as a bearer.

s3.4
00:00:48.707 --> 00:00:51.597
The gateway checks the token, the server, and the role.

s3.5
00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:55.477
If any check fails, it answers 403, and never says which check.

s3.6
00:00:55.477 --> 00:00:59.460
Otherwise it drops the agent's token, and adds the stored secret headers.

s3.7
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.986
The token is revoked when the session ends, or expires after four hours.

s4.1
00:01:04.336 --> 00:01:07.097
Symphony ignores every other MCP config.

s4.2
00:01:07.097 --> 00:01:13.444
The agent's settings turn project configs off, so a repository's own .omp/mcp.json has no effect.

s4.3
00:01:13.444 --> 00:01:18.574
They also set tools.xdev to false, so a read-only role gains no write tool.

s4.4
00:01:18.574 --> 00:01:23.916
A stdio server gets no secret headers, and no env variable that looks like a credential.

s4.5
00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:27.643
Its command must exist in the image where the agent runs.

s5.1
00:01:27.993 --> 00:01:33.023
In local mode, the engines keep the host's omp agent dir, with its logins.

s5.2
00:01:33.023 --> 00:01:37.372
Symphony writes a plugin and a settings overlay into the attempt's private dir.

s5.3
00:01:37.372 --> 00:01:40.673
The engine starts with --plugin-dir and --config.

s5.4
00:01:40.673 --> 00:01:45.431
The overlay wins over the host's settings, so the repository config stays off.

s6.1
00:01:45.781 --> 00:01:49.025
Admins add servers in Tern, under Admin, MCP.

s6.2
00:01:49.025 --> 00:01:52.593
Each row shows the transport, the roles, the headers and the state.

s6.3
00:01:52.593 --> 00:01:56.962
Secret headers are write-only. Tern shows their names, never their values.

s6.4
00:01:56.962 --> 00:02:00.198
Test connects through the gateway, and lists the tools or the error.

s6.5
00:02:00.198 --> 00:02:03.923
The CLI does the same, with set, headers, test and list.

s6.6
00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:08.871
Headers come as JSON on standard input, so no value appears in an argument.

s7.1
00:02:09.221 --> 00:02:12.181
On Kubernetes, the gateway reads a mounted Secret.

s7.2
00:02:12.181 --> 00:02:16.413
The kubelet updates it after about a minute, so a change can take a minute to work.

s7.3
00:02:16.413 --> 00:02:19.392
A running agent keeps its servers until its session ends.

s7.4
00:02:19.392 --> 00:02:24.766
In local mode, your own ~/.omp/agent/mcp.json still loads.

s8.1
00:02:25.116 --> 00:02:29.654
Scenario 12 of the mocked certification ran MCP through the gateway.

s8.2
00:02:29.654 --> 00:02:34.594
An admin stored a mock server with two secret headers, and its test listed two tools.

s8.3
00:02:34.594 --> 00:02:38.280
The repository named its own server too. It got no request.

s8.4
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.915
The coding agent called the tool, and the result reached the ticket in 16 seconds.

s8.5
00:02:42.915 --> 00:02:46.016
The mock saw the stored headers, and never the agent's token.

s8.6
00:02:46.016 --> 00:02:49.218
At the end, the revoked token got 403 at the gateway.

s8.7
00:02:49.218 --> 00:02:53.582
The assistant used the same route, and answered on Slack in 9 seconds.
